= Schema — Technical =

== Relational schema ==
A relational schema can be modeled as relation symbols with attribute domains plus integrity constraints.

Typical constraints:
* primary keys;
* foreign keys;
* functional dependencies;
* CHECK predicates;
* uniqueness;
* nullability.

These are usually interpreted under database state/closed-world conventions.

== Structural schemas ==
Document schemas constrain syntax trees or value structures.

Examples:
* XML Schema;
* JSON Schema;
* Protocol Buffers.

Validation asks whether an instance belongs to the language defined by the schema.

== Semantic schema ==
RDFS and ontology languages attach formal meaning to classes/properties.

For example:

<pre>
p rdfs:domain C
x p y
→ x rdf:type C
</pre>

This is inference, not failure reporting.

== Shape constraints ==
SHACL shape:

<pre>
PersonShape
  targetClass Person
  path birthDate
  minCount 1
  datatype xsd:date
</pre>

describes graph conformance expectations.

It can coexist with an open-world RDF/OWL ontology.

== Evolution compatibility ==
Schema migration can be:
* backward compatible;
* forward compatible;
* breaking.

Compatibility depends on producer/consumer assumptions rather than version numbers alone.

